\name{PheatmapAnnotationLayerProcessor}
\alias{PheatmapAnnotationLayerProcessor}
\title{pheatmap Annotation Track Layer Processor}
\description{
Reads one annotation track of a pheatmap -- one variable of
\code{annotation_row} or \code{annotation_col}, found by
\code{\link[=pheatmap_annotation_tracks]{pheatmap_annotation_tracks()}} -- as a layer of the heat layer's own
subplot, after it, named by the variable (\code{name}).

\strong{Shape.} Every track is one row of cells, one per column (a column
annotation) or per row (a row annotation) of the heat layer, in the
order the heat layer reads them, a row track's top first. Its \code{x}
labels are the heat layer's column or row labels, so switching layers
from a heat cell to a column track keeps the column.
\itemize{
\item A numeric track is a \code{heat} layer of one row: \code{y} the variable's
name, \code{points} its values, exactly; a value with no colour drawn is a
hole.
\item A categorical track is a \code{step} layer of one series, one point per
cell: \code{x} its label, \code{y} the level's place among the levels (the
order of \code{annotation_colors}, or else the factor's), and \code{label} the
level, which maidr.js announces in place of \code{y}. A missing value is
\code{y: null} with no \code{label}. maidr.js's \code{heat} cell is a number only, so a
level can be announced only by a trace that carries a name beside its
number.
}

The ComplexHeatmap reader emits its tracks in the same shape
(\code{ComplexHeatmapAnnotationLayerProcessor}, #392).

\strong{Selectors.} Each cell is the rect it is drawn as, in the grob named
\link{PHEATMAP_ANNOTATION_GROBS}: for \code{heat}, a one-row grid; for \code{step}, one
selector listing every cell, which match in drawn order. A track whose
rects are not one per value, placed in drawn order, has none.
}
